A||N||11||polomeno||Best place to buy door locks??||||1||
Z||000000||polomeno||08-01-2001||08:29 PM||||Hey guys, I have an 84 coupe and need new door locks, where is the best place?<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000001||Lisa 86 2m4||08-01-2001||09:51 PM||||Hi John,<BR>If you are only looking for the lock cylinders most all auto parts stores should carry them or try the GM dealerships. If you don't mind paying $10 more order them from the Fiero Store.  Bewarned, changing the cylinders is not as easy as it may seem. The steps are easy, the process is....'frustrating'!! Hope this helps!<BR>Lisa ||||reg||1
Z||000002||sloth85GT||08-02-2001||12:46 AM||||No kidding Lisa, I spent 4 hours doing my drivers side, it's pure HELL!!! Then the clip that holds the linkage onto the cylinder fell off, that was hell again to put back on. I got my locks on Ebay for $15.00. So good luck with the installation, prepare to put a whole day aside to do it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif"> <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT||||reg||8
Z||000003||fierohoho||08-02-2001||08:16 AM||||The kits with the door locks, do they include the trunk lock as well?<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
Z||000004||Lisa 86 2m4||08-02-2001||07:39 PM||||The lock sets I have come across are for the doors only. I have seen the trunk lock sold sperately. And that usually costs the same as the two door locks. Go figure.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jim Jimenez||08-03-2001||12:22 AM||||You could send me your trunk key, and I will code new door locks to match,just like when the car was new.<P>If you don't want to do that, Every GM dealer has someone who codes locks. Keep asking until you find one who wouldn't grumble and will do it for a agreeable price.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Posthumane||08-03-2001||04:26 PM||||On my 84 SE, the left side lock has the outside ring busted off and springs and stuff floating around inside, and the passenger side doesn't open. Is there anything more that i would have to replace, or just the cylinders?||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||08-03-2001||05:39 PM||||I've gotten them from both the dealer and just about any locksmith.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Lisa 86 2m4||08-04-2001||10:51 PM||||Posthumane,<BR>The outside ring is a part of the cylinder itself, so that should be all that you need to do. The cylinder is a self contained unit so either it works or it doesn't. I guess that can be a good thing. So, the springs and stuff are all parts of the cylinders.||||reg||1
Z||000009||MD_Fiero||08-04-2001||11:32 PM||||Man, will fiero store ever be up again? erm!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||JayUSA77||08-05-2001||01:14 AM||||i got power door locks for sale made by spal. i couldnt figure out how to use them but if you could i would part for them for 45 bucks not including shipping. I paid 75 so its a good deal. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>j<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||GT-maniac||08-05-2001||03:30 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by sloth85GT:<BR>[B]No kidding Lisa, I spent 4 hours doing my drivers side, it's pure HELL!!! Then the clip that holds the linkage onto the cylinder fell off, that was hell again to put back on. I got my locks on Ebay for $15.00. So good luck with the installation, prepare to put a whole day aside to do it.<P>I just did this on my 87 and after wasting half a day trying to get the little clip back in with a pair of needle nose pliers from the inside of the door I decided to do it the easy way. It's far far easier just to take the door skin off. They're easy to take off and when it's off whole lock assembly is open for you to put the clip in. The only tricky part is getting the rod into the right spot when you put it back on. It took me about 45 minutes tops to do both doors.<BR>||||reg||1
